#{[#edb/ref 17592186050574 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567} :activity/id #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :tutorial/title "Continuity at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568}}] :activity/title "Determining Continuity from Graphs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068989}] [#edb/ref 17592186050608 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797} :activity/id #uuid "22d6d34d-749f-599b-a696-f6952b1b29b5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088944 :tutorial/content "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088945 :tutorial/content "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088862 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." :tutorial/title "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087962}] [#edb/ref 17592186050690 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097419} :activity/id #uuid "a08f28ad-f5a0-5009-8802-2bc2d37a5e98" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097816 :tutorial/content "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}   & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)'\\  \n                                                    & = 5 (\\ln x)'\\\n                                                    &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n                                                    &= \\dfrac{5}{x}.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t  \n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097421}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :question/id #uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" :question/math-academy-id "e-3628" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\ln x+x^2-1$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)'\\\n\t\t\t&=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0\\\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097817 :tutorial/content "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{\\operatorname{ln}a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function with a General Base"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097423}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :question/id #uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" :question/math-academy-id "e-3629" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e.$ By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096416 :question/id #uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3630" :question/problem "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right)\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x )\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{1}{xln⁡4}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ln⁡4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096894}] [#edb/ref 17592186050601 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129995} :activity/id #uuid "08d5ebdb-d36f-5ada-8dab-70cc77c19c31" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130363 :tutorial/content "It's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph. We summarize all possible cases in the table below:\n\n| Multiplicity | Case 1 | Case 2 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The graph is **tangent** to the $x$-axis at multiple roots. This means that if $x=a$ is a multiple root, then the graph of the polynomial is parallel to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a.$\n\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd* (including a simple root of multiplicity $1$), the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a.$\n\n- The graph of the polynomial is *never* tangent to the $x$-axis at simple roots." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130184}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128960 :question/id #uuid "2f03be7e-0a77-5869-b591-6a8f8f3942de" :question/math-academy-id "e-5814" :question/problem "![](images/59/5970275bd70bcfd944c03abc8f60c2b159e3545dc876a0dd05bc23343b85bdec.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial above?\n\n- $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\n- $y=(x+2)^3(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\n- $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x =-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x =-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x = 2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y= (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129147 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128963 :question/id #uuid "e158501f-0bac-5d16-956a-59da7f82620f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5818" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc5d6aa3a8afed8ebcf49b6d884eee8a443bc759367b624bc7a92cfae515863.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y = (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=\\pm 2$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\textbf{I}$  and $\\textbf{III}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$  approaches $\\infty,$ $y$  approaches  $\\infty.$\n\nOnly graph $\\textbf{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is **$\\textbf{III}$**."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128972 :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d6f70aed4d013bfcdd9060124ad003e3e1ed6ce70852e4f60d71cdd8d65cc4.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\n- $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\n- $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129078 :question/id #uuid "53aa15a0-8633-51b8-8731-9099eec94940" :question/math-academy-id "e-9941" :question/problem "![](images/26/2683c694582a7c2dbe1c95cce6af22ef941a1e77b54721151a935e81ec2b3b1a.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity\n\n- $x=-4$ is a simple root" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-4.$ So, $x=-4$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-4.$ So, $x=-4$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130009}}] :activity/title "Graphing General Polynomials" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129382}]}
